1 Create a backup of your Music folder (or any folder with at least 3 files) using the tar command and compress it with gzip, saving the output as music_backup.tar.gz in your home directory.
=>tar -czvf ~/music_backup.tar.gz Music/
  Music/
Music/song1.mp3
Music/song2.mp3
Music/cover.jpg
Music/lyrics.txt

2. Extract the contents of a tar.gz file (for example, music_backup.tar.gz) into a new directory called restored_music and verify that all files are restored correctly.
=>mkdir -p ~/restored_music
tar -xzvf ~/music_backup.tar.gz -C ~/restored_music
diff -r ~/Music ~/restored_music/Music
All 4 files restored correctly under ~/restored_music/Music/.

3. Write a shell command that creates a compressed backup of all .jpg images from your Downloads folder into a file named images_backup.tar.gz, excluding any files larger than 5MB.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the find command with size filtering and tar with --files-from.</em>
=>find ~/Downloads -maxdepth 1 -name "*.jpg" -size -5M -print > /tmp/jpg_list.txt
tar -czvf ~/images_backup.tar.gz --files-from=/tmp/jpg_list.txt
Included: photo1.jpg, photo2.jpg, photo3.jpg
Excluded: large_photo.jpg (6 MB)
Archive: /root/images_backup.tar.gz

4. Simulate a restore scenario: delete one file from your original Music folder, then use your music_backup.tar.gz archive to restore only the deleted file without overwriting the rest.
=>rm ~/Music/song2.mp3    
tar -xzvf ~/music_backup.tar.gz -C ~/ Music/song2.mp3

Before restore: song2.mp3 was missing
After restore: song2.mp3 is back with original content (“Song 2 content”)
Other files in ~/Music were left untouched
